2. ArrayList vs LinkedList

ArrayList

ArrayList is backed by a dynamically resizable array.

Maintains insertion order.

get(index) → O(1)

Append at end → O(1) amortized

Insert/remove in the middle → generally O(n) because elements may shift.

Good cache locality and relatively low memory overhead.

LinkedList

Java's LinkedList is a doubly linked list.

Conceptually:

Node A <-> Node B <-> Node C

Each node contains the element and references to neighboring nodes.

Maintains insertion order.

get(index) → O(n)

Insertion/removal can be O(1) when the relevant node is already known.

Finding an indexed position can still be O(n).

Comparison

Operation

ArrayList

LinkedList

get(index)

O(1)

O(n)

add(element) at end

O(1) amortized

O(1)

add(0, element)

O(n)

O(1)

Remove first element

O(n)

O(1)

Random access

Excellent

Poor

Interview answer

ArrayList is backed by a dynamically resizable array, so it provides O(1) random access but insertion/removal in the middle is O(n) because elements may need to be shifted. LinkedList is a doubly linked list, so indexed access is O(n), while insertion/removal can be O(1) when the relevant node is already available.

Important: Do not simply say "LinkedList is better for insertion." The precise statement is that it is efficient when the relevant node/position is already known.

3. ArrayList Capacity, Size & Resizing

Size vs Capacity

size     = number of elements currently stored
capacity = space currently available in the backing array

Example:

size = 5
capacity = 8

Resizing

Java arrays have fixed length. When an ArrayList's backing array is full:

A larger array is allocated.

Existing elements are copied.

The new element is inserted.

The old backing array becomes eligible for garbage collection.

Therefore:

A resize can cost O(n).

Normal append is O(1).

Across many additions, append is O(1) amortized.

Pre-sizing

If you know approximately how many elements are needed:

List<Transaction> transactions =
        new ArrayList<>(1_000_000);

This sets initial capacity, not size.

Immediately after creation:

capacity ≈ 1,000,000
size = 0

Pre-sizing can reduce repeated resizing and copying.

4. HashSet

HashSet is useful for:

Unique elements.

Fast membership checking.

Average O(1) insertion, removal, and lookup.

Example:

Set<String> processedIds = new HashSet<>();

processedIds.add("TX123");
processedIds.add("TX456");
processedIds.add("TX123");

The duplicate TX123 is not stored twice.

HashSet vs HashMap

HashSet → unique values
HashMap → key-value pairs

Conceptually, HashSet uses a HashMap internally and stores the set element as a key with a dummy value.

No random access

This is invalid:

set.get(5); // no indexed access

Instead:

set.contains("TX123");

is average O(1).

Banking example

For one million transaction IDs, if the main question is:

"Have I already processed this transaction?"

Use a HashSet, because:

HashSet.contains()  → O(1) average
ArrayList.contains() → O(n)

Do not claim HashSet operations are always O(1); average-case complexity assumes good hash distribution.

5. String Pool and == vs equals()

Consider:

String a = "hello";
String b = "hello";
String c = new String("hello");

Conceptually:

String Pool:
"hello" <- a
          b

Heap:
"hello" <- c

Therefore:

a == b       // true
a == c       // false
b == c       // false

== compares object references.

But:

a.equals(b)  // true
a.equals(c)  // true
b.equals(c)  // true

String.equals() compares logical content.

Interview answer

== compares whether two references point to the same object, while equals() compares logical content. String literals may be shared through the String Pool, while new String() creates a separate String object.

6. String Immutability

String objects are immutable.

String s = "hello";
s.concat(" world");

System.out.println(s);

Output:

hello

concat() creates a new String instead of modifying the existing String.

To use the new value:

s = s.concat(" world");

Why String immutability matters

Safe sharing through the String Pool.

Naturally thread-safe.

Useful for security-sensitive values.

Hash codes can safely be used/cached because content does not change.

Reassignment:

String s = "hello";
s = "world";

does not modify "hello"; it changes what s references.

7. Checked vs Unchecked Exceptions

Checked exceptions

The compiler requires checked exceptions to be handled or declared.

Examples:

IOException
SQLException
FileNotFoundException

Example:

public void readFile() throws IOException {
    // ...
}

Unchecked exceptions

Unchecked exceptions extend RuntimeException.

Examples:

NullPointerException
IllegalArgumentException
NumberFormatException
ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException

The compiler does not force explicit catch/declare handling.

Important distinction

A syntax error is a compile-time error, not a checked exception.

Compile-time error != checked exception

Interview answer

Checked exceptions must be handled or declared by the compiler, such as IOException and SQLException. Unchecked exceptions extend RuntimeException and do not require explicit handling, such as NullPointerException and IllegalArgumentException.

8. final, finally, and finalize

final

Used to restrict reassignment, overriding, or inheritance.

final int x = 10;

The variable cannot be reassigned.

final void process() {}

A subclass cannot override the method.

final class PaymentService {}

The class cannot be extended.

finally

Used with exception handling, commonly for cleanup:

try {
    processPayment();
} catch (Exception e) {
    handleError(e);
} finally {
    closeConnection();
}

Modern Java generally prefers try-with-resources for resource cleanup.

finalize()

An old garbage-collection-related cleanup mechanism.

For modern Java:

Do not rely on finalization.

It is deprecated.

Prefer try-with-resources or explicit resource management.

Memory trick

final     → restriction
finally   → cleanup block
finalize  → old GC-related mechanism; avoid using it

9. Generics

Generics specify the type a collection works with.

List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();

This gives compile-time type safety:

names.add("Dinesh");
names.add(100); // compile-time error

Without generics:

List names = new ArrayList();

names.add("Dinesh");
names.add(100);

String name = (String) names.get(1);

This can cause ClassCastException at runtime.

Benefits

Compile-time type safety.

Fewer casts.

Earlier error detection.

Better readability.

10. Raw Types vs Wildcards

These are different:

List          // raw type
List<?>       // wildcard
List<String>  // parameterized type

List<?> means a list of some unknown type.

It is not the same as a raw List.

11. Generic Invariance

Even though:

Integer extends Number

this is invalid:

List<Integer> integers = new ArrayList<>();
List<Number> numbers = integers; // compile-time error

Java generics are invariant.

Why?

If this were allowed:

List<Integer> integers = new ArrayList<>();
List<Number> numbers = integers;
numbers.add(10.5); // Double is a Number

A Double could enter a list that is supposed to contain only Integer.

Therefore Java prevents the assignment.

12. PECS — Producer Extends, Consumer Super

extends → producer / read

List<? extends Number> numbers

You can safely read:

Number n = numbers.get(0);

You generally cannot add a Number because the actual list could be List<Double>.

super → consumer / write

List<? super Integer> numbers

You can safely add:

numbers.add(10);

The underlying list could be:

List<Integer>
List<Number>
List<Object>

Key rule

Do not think:

Producer = parent, Consumer = child.

PECS is about data-flow direction:

? extends T → read / producer
? super T   → write / consumer

13. Immutable Classes

An immutable object is an object whose state cannot change after construction.

Typical design:

Make the class final.

Make fields private final.

Initialize fields through a constructor.

Do not provide setters.

Use defensive copies for mutable fields.

Example:

public final class Employee {

    private final String name;
    private final int salary;

    public Employee(String name, int salary) {
        this.name = name;
        this.salary = salary;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public int getSalary() {
        return salary;
    }

    public int calculateBonus() {
        return salary * 10 / 100;
    }
}

Why final class?

Immutability does not mathematically require final in every possible design, but making the class final prevents subclassing from introducing mutable state or changing behavior, making the immutability guarantee safer.

Logic methods are allowed

An immutable class can contain normal business logic:

public int calculateBonus() {
    return salary * 10 / 100;
}

It does not need to be final if the class itself is final.

Singleton is NOT required

Immutable → state cannot change
Singleton  → only one instance should exist

An immutable class can have many instances.

Mutable fields need defensive copying

This alone is not enough:

private final List<String> skills;

final prevents reassignment of the reference; it does not make the List immutable.

Use:

this.skills = List.copyOf(skills);

when appropriate.

14. Constructor Rule

Java automatically provides an implicit no-argument constructor only if no constructor is declared.

This:

class Employee {
}

gets an implicit no-arg constructor.

But this:

class Employee {

    Employee(String name) {
        // ...
    }
}

does NOT automatically get Employee().

If both are needed, define both explicitly.

For immutable classes, a parameterized constructor is commonly used to initialize all final fields.

15. Kafka Fundamentals

Kafka is an event-streaming platform used for high-volume asynchronous processing.

For this Java/Spring Boot role, the goal is application-level Kafka understanding rather than Kafka platform administration.

Core architecture

Producer
   ↓
Topic
   ↓
Partitions
   ↓
Consumer Group
   ↓
Consumers

Topic

A named stream/category of records.

Example:

transaction-events

Partition

Topics are divided into partitions for parallelism and scalability.

Topic: transaction-events

Partition 0
Partition 1
Partition 2

Kafka ordering is guaranteed within a partition, not globally across all partitions.

Offset

An offset is the position of a record within a specific partition.

Each partition has its own offset sequence.

Offset is not a global index across the topic.

Consumer group

Consumers in the same group work together.

Within one consumer group:

Only one consumer actively consumes a particular partition at a time.

Example:

3 partitions
3 consumers

P0 → C1
P1 → C2
P2 → C3

If there are 5 consumers for 3 partitions:

P0 → C1
P1 → C2
P2 → C3

C4 → idle
C5 → idle

Consumers from different groups can independently consume the same partition.

16. Kafka Offsets and Replay

Do not confuse:

Current consumer position
vs
Committed offset

A committed offset represents the consumer group's recorded progress for a topic-partition.

After a consumer crash/rebalance, another consumer can resume from the group's committed progress.

Kafka retention and offsets are separate.

If records are still retained, consumers can potentially replay them by resetting offsets.

17. Kafka Ordering and Keys

Kafka guarantees ordering within a partition.

If ordering for a business entity matters, use a stable key so related events are routed to the same partition.

Example:

transactionId → partitioning key

This can preserve ordering for events associated with the same transaction/entity.

18. Kafka Consumer Lag

Consumer lag represents how far behind a consumer group is from the latest available records.

High lag can indicate:

Consumers are too slow.

Processing is expensive.

Traffic increased.

Downstream dependencies are slow.

Consumer failures/rebalances.

Insufficient parallelism.

Production troubleshooting often starts by checking lag and processing throughput.

19. Kafka Delivery Semantics

At-least-once

Receive
  ↓
Process
  ↓
Commit

If the application crashes after processing but before commit, the message may be processed again.

Therefore duplicates are possible.

At-most-once

Receive
  ↓
Commit
  ↓
Process

If the application crashes after commit but before processing, the message may be lost.

Exactly-once

Requires more sophisticated transactional/idempotent design.

Do not casually claim exactly-once semantics for ordinary consumers.

20. Kafka Idempotency

At-least-once processing often requires idempotent business logic.

Example:

transactionId = TX123

A database uniqueness constraint can prevent duplicate business effects:

transaction_id UNIQUE

Important distinction:

Kafka producer idempotence helps prevent duplicate records caused by producer retries.

It does NOT automatically make external database/business operations idempotent.

A timestamp helps audit/order tracking but does not itself provide idempotency.

21. Kafka Interview Positioning

A safe and accurate interview statement:

I have hands-on experience working with Kafka-based asynchronous systems, including high-volume transaction streams, consumer groups, consumer lag, offset management and replay, and downstream processing. I'm familiar with the Spring Kafka programming model and underlying Kafka concepts, although my primary role was not Kafka platform administration.

